# The logarithmic search depth of associative memory in the hyperbolic bulk, machine-checked by `seed test`. A
# content-addressable query broadcasts to a region that grows exponentially with the number of steps (the hyperbolic
# shells grow exponentially, see the pentagrid and hyperbolic files), so a match among N cells is found in only about
# log N broadcast steps. The some-or-none signal makes each step constant-time. These exponential-reach counts are the
# arithmetic face of "associative search is logarithmic in the bulk".

# A binary-branching query reaches 2^4 = 16 cells in four steps. A wrong reach count breaks the product.
hold search-reaches-sixteen-cells-in-four-steps
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2
      call multiply
        code 2
        code 2
    code 16

# Doubling the depth squares the reach: eight steps reach 2^8 = 256 cells, the square of the four-step reach
# (16 * 16 = 256). So linear depth buys exponential coverage.
hold doubling-depth-squares-reach
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 16
      code 16
    code 256

# The 24-direction bulk reaches far more per step than a binary tree: with up to 24 neighbors, one step covers 24 cells,
# 24 * 24 = 576 in two steps. The high branching is why the hyperbolic mesh has such small diameter.
hold bulk-reaches-by-twenty-four-per-step
  call is-equal
    call multiply
      code 24
      code 24
    code 576
